{"event":"join","runId":"briefing-proof3-2026-11-16","forkId":"briefing-proof3-2026-11-16/fork-0","outcome":[{"ok":true,"value":{"ok":true,"error":"","id":"2611.14196","title":"Joint Quantization and Dynamic Partitioning for Context-Aware Edge Vision Transformers","authors":"Zhu Jiaqi , Jain Prakriti , Katare Dewant , Kumar Saurabh","submitted":"10 Nov 2026","abstract":"Deploying vision transformer models across connected and automated vehicles (CAVs) requires joint management of computation and Vehicle-to-Vehicle (V2V) communication. Static split-inference methods use fixed partition points and cannot adapt to time-varying link bandwidth, processing load, and concurrent multi-stream traffic. This paper proposes an edge orchestration framework that jointly selects model partition points, per-layer quantization bitwidths, and execution placement across heterogeneous vehicular compute nodes. The joint partitioning and mixed-precision allocation problem is formulated as a Mixed-Integer Linear Program (MILP) that minimizes compute and communication energy subject to end-to-end latency and task-quality constraints. The framework is evaluated on Jetson Orin Nano (8~GB), Jetson Orin NX (16~GB), and Jetson AGX Orin (32~GB) nodes using OPV2V-driven multi-stream workloads with $K \\in \\{2,4,6,8\\}$. Across the evaluated configurations, the proposed formulation reduces V2V communication energy by $33.3$--$36.0\\%$ relative to a Uniform INT8 split. At $K=8$, end-to-end task completion latency is reduced by $34.0\\%$ on the Orin Nano and $33.1\\%$ on the Orin NX relative to the same baseline, while limiting the reduction in 3D detection accuracy to $3.1$--$4.1$ percentage points in $\\mathrm{mAP}_{50}$ relative to FP16 execution.","numbers":["Across the evaluated configurations, the proposed formulation reduces V2V communication energy by 33.3–36.0% relative to a Uniform INT8 split.","At K=8, end-to-end task completion latency is reduced by 34.0% on the Orin Nano and 33.1% on the Orin NX relative to the same baseline, while limiting the reduction in 3D detection accuracy to 3.1–4.1 percentage points in mAP_50 relative to FP16 execution."],"method":["This paper proposes an edge orchestration framework that jointly selects model partition points, per-layer quantization bitwidths, and execution placement across heterogeneous vehicular compute nodes."],"caveats":[],"topics":["architecture-attention","vision-multimodal","quantization"],"score":10,"fullText":true,"readChars":6225,"absUrl":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14196","htmlUrl":"https://ar5iv.org/html/2611.14196"}},{"ok":true,"value":{"ok":true,"error":"","id":"2611.11320","title":"Beyond Similarity Entropy: A Rank-Guided Data-Free Quantization Framework for Vision Transformers","authors":"Runqing Jiang , Ye Zhang , Pengpeng Yu , Zhuo Song , Changjun Li , Longguang Wang , Yulan Guo","submitted":"8 Nov 2026","abstract":"Vision Transformers (ViTs) have demonstrated remarkable performance, yet their computational and memory demands pose significant challenges for deployment on mobile devices. Data-free quantization (DFQ) enables the compression of ViTs without access to their original training data. Existing DFQ methods often synthesize calibration samples by maximizing the entropy of patch similarity. However, similarity entropy describes the dispersion of similarity values without ensuring that the generated samples cover diverse feature directions, potentially limiting their effectiveness for quantization calibration. To address this limitation, we propose RankQ-ViT, a rank-guided DFQ framework for ViTs. We first observe that patch similarity matrices derived from Gaussian noise tend to have lower ranks than those derived from real images. Motivated by this observation, we introduce a block-aware self-contrastive objective that promotes feature diversity during synthetic data generation without explicitly computing matrix rank at each optimization step. We then select calibration samples based on their rank and semantic consistency and use them for block-wise reconstruction of the quantized model. Experiments on ImageNet, COCO, ModelNet40, and ShapeNet demonstrate the effectiveness of RankQ-ViT across multiple architectures and tasks, particularly under low-bit quantization. The code is available at this https URL","numbers":[],"method":["To address this limitation, we propose RankQ-ViT, a rank-guided DFQ framework for ViTs.","Motivated by this observation, we introduce a block-aware self-contrastive objective that promotes feature diversity during synthetic data generation without explicitly computing matrix rank at each optimization step."],"caveats":["However, similarity entropy describes the dispersion of similarity values without ensuring that the generated samples cover diverse feature directions, potentially limiting their effectiveness for quantization calibration.","To address this limitation, we propose RankQ-ViT, a rank-guided DFQ framework for ViTs."],"topics":["architecture-attention","vision-multimodal","quantization"],"score":12,"fullText":true,"readChars":6453,"absUrl":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11320","htmlUrl":"https://ar5iv.org/html/2611.11320"}},{"ok":true,"value":{"ok":true,"error":"","id":"2611.11890","title":"Training Vision Transformers to Focus: Emergence of Spatial Attention Head Specialization","authors":"Yuji Nozawa , Yu-Chieh Lin , Youyang Ng","submitted":"9 Nov 2026","abstract":"Self-supervised learning in Vision Transformer models, such as DINO, enables robust visual perception without requiring labeled data by leveraging large-scale image datasets. However, these models lack a focusing mechanism during training, resulting in perceptual representations that are often ambiguous or overly similar. In this work, we introduce two visual focusing loss functions designed to establish correspondence between the model's attention and specific regions within images. Specifically, we leverage the multi-head attention mechanism within the model to selectively steer the model's focus, leading to the emergence of spatial attention head specialization, characterized by diverse and spatially focused perceptual capabilities without requiring annotations. Through both qualitative and quantitative evaluations, we demonstrate that our method substantially increases the spatial selectivity and diversity of attention heads, improving both interpretability and performance on dense prediction tasks such as zero-shot image segmentation.","numbers":[],"method":["In this work, we introduce two visual focusing loss functions designed to establish correspondence between the model's attention and specific regions within images.","Through both qualitative and quantitative evaluations, we demonstrate that our method substantially increases the spatial selectivity and diversity of attention heads, improving both interpretability and performance on dense prediction tasks such as zero-shot image segmentation."],"caveats":["However, these models lack a focusing mechanism during training, resulting in perceptual representations that are often ambiguous or overly similar."],"topics":["architecture-attention","vision-multimodal"],"score":10,"fullText":true,"readChars":5692,"absUrl":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11890","htmlUrl":"https://ar5iv.org/html/2611.11890"}},{"ok":true,"value":{"ok":true,"error":"","id":"2611.08669","title":"ChainFlow: Few-Step Latent Flow Matching for Speculative Decoding","authors":"Selim Aktas , Yusuf Erdem Erdem , Ahmed Zeer , Himmet Toprak Kesgin , Muzaffer Kaan Yuce , Mehmet Fatih Amasyali","submitted":"5 Nov 2026","abstract":"Speculative decoding has become a standard way to accelerate LLM inference: a lightweight drafter proposes tokens that the target verifies in one parallel pass. Existing drafters face trade-offs that limits their speedups: autoregressive drafters generate tokens one at a time, so drafting cost grows with draft length, while diffusion drafters generate tokens in parallel but predict each position independently, ignoring inter-token dependencies. We introduce ChainFlow, a new speculative decoding paradigm and the first flow-matching drafter conditioned on the target's hidden states. ChainFlow treats drafting as a short transport over hidden states, needing one or two flow steps instead of the many denoising steps of generation from noise. A variational autoencoder compresses these states so drafting cost barely grows with target size, lightweight feedback heads restore inter-token dependencies, and tree drafting raises acceptance. On Qwen3.5 models, at matched drafter size and training data, ChainFlow outperforms EAGLE-3 and DFlash in vLLM by 39% and 11%, respectively. These gains further widen as drafters are adapted to new languages sequentially. Our ablations cover drafter design and concurrent serving, and we open-source our code and drafter models.","numbers":["On Qwen3.5 models, at matched drafter size and training data, ChainFlow outperforms EAGLE-3 and DFlash in vLLM by 39% and 11%, respectively."],"method":["We introduce ChainFlow, a new speculative decoding paradigm and the first flow-matching drafter conditioned on the target's hidden states."],"caveats":[],"topics":["diffusion","speculative-decoding"],"score":12,"fullText":true,"readChars":6042,"absUrl":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08669","htmlUrl":"https://ar5iv.org/html/2611.08669"}},{"ok":true,"value":{"ok":true,"error":"","id":"2611.17797","title":"Vision-as-a-Tool: Visual Harness for Multimodal Agentic Coding","authors":"Haolin Yang , Wenshan Wu , Jin Jiang , Yu Cheng , Yan Xia , Ting Song , Yangyu Huang , Zhiliang Peng , Wenhui Wang , Hangbo Bao , Yuzhe Zhang , Wen-Da Wei , Tengchao Lv , Li Dong , Lei Cui , Furu Wei","submitted":"13 Nov 2026","abstract":"Multimodal agentic coding requires robust visual reasoning to analyze observations and evaluate generated artifacts. Current frameworks largely rely on a Thinking-with-Images paradigm, where agents depend on their intrinsic vision to execute visually grounded tasks. However, this architectural coupling introduces a fundamental bottleneck. Agent performance remains strictly bounded by internal perceptual limitations. Furthermore, text-only agents lack native perception. Without specialized tools, they cannot perform visual reasoning. To overcome these constraints, we introduce the Vision-as-a-Tool (VaaT) paradigm. Prior visual tool-use merely manipulates images for an agent's intrinsic vision to read. In contrast, VaaT reframes general-purpose multimodal large language models (MLLMs) as callable external utilities. As a result, text-only agents can completely offload visual reasoning during agentic execution. Meanwhile, vision-capable agents can dynamically augment their native perception on demand. We instantiate this paradigm in VHarness, a unified framework driven by five atomic actions. We comprehensively evaluate VHarness across Image-to-HTML, Image-to-SVG, and Image-to-3D generation tasks. Experimental results demonstrate that VHarness consistently improves visual fidelity and geometric accuracy across both text-only and multimodal coding agents. These findings highlight that modularizing visual reasoning into callable utilities provides a flexible and scalable foundation for future agentic coding systems.","numbers":[],"method":["To overcome these constraints, we introduce the Vision-as-a-Tool (VaaT) paradigm."],"caveats":["However, this architectural coupling introduces a fundamental bottleneck.","Agent performance remains strictly bounded by internal perceptual limitations."],"topics":["vision-multimodal"],"score":6,"fullText":true,"readChars":6755,"absUrl":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17797","htmlUrl":"https://ar5iv.org/html/2611.17797"}},{"ok":true,"value":{"ok":true,"error":"","id":"2611.17930","title":"Token2Tile: Token-Wise Sparse Attention with Tile Execution through a Low-Rank Space for Video DiTs","authors":"Xuecheng Wu , Aichen Feng , Jinyan Chen , Qingjie Zhao , Julien Lai","submitted":"13 Nov 2026","abstract":"Attention is essential for modeling spatio-temporal dependencies in video diffusion transformers, but its quadratic cost makes it a major inference bottleneck. Its high sparsity offers an opportunity to accelerate generation by focusing computation on important interactions. However, accuracy and execution favor different granularities: each query attends to its own key tokens, whereas GPU kernels work on tiles. We present Token2Tile, a training-free sparse attention operator that combines per-query scoring and a per-query residual with tile execution. An online low-rank space, computed directly from queries and keys to preserve attention logits, makes clustering, scoring, and residual approximation efficient. Token2Tile unites query-specific selections to allocate exact work across query clusters, then packs the selected keys into GPU tiles. On MiniMax-H3 and Wan2.1-14B, Token2Tile delivers 1.48-1.54x end-to-end speedup over dense FlashAttention-3 with VBench scores within 0.5 points of dense. At comparable speeds on H3, it improves PSNR over SOL and SubBlock by 5.5-6.8 dB; on Wan, it exceeds SOL by 4.6-4.8 dB while running faster.","numbers":["On MiniMax-H3 and Wan2.1-14B, Token2Tile delivers 1.48-1.54x end-to-end speedup over dense FlashAttention-3 with VBench scores within 0.5 points of dense.","At comparable speeds on H3, it improves PSNR over SOL and SubBlock by 5.5-6.8 dB; on Wan, it exceeds SOL by 4.6-4.8 dB while running faster."],"method":["We present Token2Tile, a training-free sparse attention operator that combines per-query scoring and a per-query residual with tile execution."],"caveats":["However, accuracy and execution favor different granularities: each query attends to its own key tokens, whereas GPU kernels work on tiles."],"topics":["architecture-attention","vision-multimodal","diffusion"],"score":10,"fullText":true,"readChars":5930,"absUrl":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17930","htmlUrl":"https://ar5iv.org/html/2611.17930"}},{"ok":true,"value":{"ok":true,"error":"","id":"2611.18057","title":"SWAM: Saliency-Weighted Asymmetric Merging for Efficient Vision Transformers","authors":"Utkarsh Kumar Singh , Manish Das , Shiv Ram Dubey , Satish Kumar Singh","submitted":"13 Nov 2026","abstract":"Vision Transformers (ViTs) achieve strong performance on image recognition tasks, yet their quadratic self-attention complexity over input tokens creates prohibitive computational costs for practical deployment. Token reduction methods offer a practical solution by progressively reducing the token sequence during the forward pass. We propose Saliency-Weighted Asymmetric Merging (SWAM), a training-based token merging framework that extends the Bipartite Soft Matching (BSM) with two saliency-aware mechanisms. We introduce a two-step matching formulation in which cosine similarity decides candidate pairs, while a multiplicative maximum saliency penalty regulates their selection for merging. We then further use asymmetric exponential saliency-weighted aggregation, which allows more salient tokens to contribute more strongly to the merged representation. A lightweight MLP-based saliency prediction module (75k parameters per stage, 1% of backbone parameters) supervised via attention rollout is trained end-to-end jointly with a DeiT-Small backbone using a composite cross-entropy, knowledge-distillation, and saliency-alignment objective. On CIFAR-100, TinyImageNet, and ImageNet-100, under a conservative schedule that reduces tokens from 196 to 100 patch tokens, with the CLS token retained throughout, 27.5% FLOPs reduction and 1.35 times speedup, SWAM matches or exceeds baseline across the reported results. Under an aggressive schedule (196 to 70 tokens with the CLS token kept throughout, 33.8% FLOPs reduction, and 1.46 times speedup), accuracy gains are achieved on ImageNet-100 (+0.23%) with minimal losses on CIFAR-100 and TinyImageNet.","numbers":["A lightweight MLP-based saliency prediction module (75k parameters per stage, 1% of backbone parameters) supervised via attention rollout is trained end-to-end jointly with a DeiT-Small backbone using a composite cross-entropy, knowledge-distillation, and saliency-alignment objective.","On CIFAR-100, TinyImageNet, and ImageNet-100, under a conservative schedule that reduces tokens from 196 to 100 patch tokens, with the CLS token retained throughout, 27.5% FLOPs reduction and 1.35 times speedup, SWAM matches or exceeds baseline across the reported results.","Under an aggressive schedule (196 to 70 tokens with the CLS token kept throughout, 33.8% FLOPs reduction, and 1.46 times speedup), accuracy gains are achieved on ImageNet-100 (+0.23%) with minimal losses on CIFAR-100 and TinyImageNet."],"method":["We propose Saliency-Weighted Asymmetric Merging (SWAM), a training-based token merging framework that extends the Bipartite Soft Matching (BSM) with two saliency-aware mechanisms.","We introduce a two-step matching formulation in which cosine similarity decides candidate pairs, while a multiplicative maximum saliency penalty regulates their selection for merging."],"caveats":[],"topics":["architecture-attention","vision-multimodal","diffusion"],"score":12,"fullText":true,"readChars":6829,"absUrl":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18057","htmlUrl":"https://ar5iv.org/html/2611.18057"}},{"ok":true,"value":{"ok":true,"error":"","id":"2611.18482","title":"MMFlash: Faster Speculative Decoding for Vision-Language Models","authors":"Sicheng Feng , Zigeng Chen , Song Wang , Gongfan Fang , Xinchao Wang","submitted":"13 Nov 2026","abstract":"Speculative decoding accelerates generation by letting a lightweight drafter propose tokens for parallel verification by a target model. Diffusion drafters such as DFlash propose an entire token block in a single forward pass, but mainly focus on text-only tasks, leaving multimodal drafting underexplored. To study this setting, we first introduce a metric of token-level visual dependence based on the KL divergence between next-token distributions with and without visual input. We find that acceptance rates decrease from 46% in the lowest visual-dependence quartile to 22% in the highest, with this disparity persisting after multimodal training. Motivated by this observation, we propose MMFlash, a simple and effective framework that adaptively upweights visually dependent tokens that the drafter predicts poorly. For long video inputs, MMFlash further employs sparse attention to reduce redundant attention computation and improve drafting quality. Across various multimodal benchmarks, MMFlash achieves lossless decoding speedups of up to $3.58\\times$, outperforming EAGLE-3 and DFlash by up to 195.4% and 17.1%, respectively.","numbers":["We find that acceptance rates decrease from 46% in the lowest visual-dependence quartile to 22% in the highest, with this disparity persisting after multimodal training.","Across various multimodal benchmarks, MMFlash achieves lossless decoding speedups of up to 3.58, outperforming EAGLE-3 and DFlash by up to 195.4% and 17.1%, respectively."],"method":["Motivated by this observation, we propose MMFlash, a simple and effective framework that adaptively upweights visually dependent tokens that the drafter predicts poorly."],"caveats":[],"topics":["architecture-attention","vision-multimodal","diffusion","speculative-decoding"],"score":18,"fullText":true,"readChars":5776,"absUrl":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18482","htmlUrl":"https://ar5iv.org/html/2611.18482"}},{"ok":true,"value":{"ok":true,"error":"","id":"2611.18549","title":"JVT: Just Video Transformer makes pixel-space video diffusion simple","authors":"Jianjun Zhou , Hua Geng , Haoyu Guo","submitted":"13 Nov 2026","abstract":"Direct diffusion modeling of high-dimensional raw video data is challenging, and existing pixel-space methods often rely on specialized architectures. We introduce JVT (Just Video Transformer), a simple large-patch DiT-style Transformer for pixel-space video diffusion, without a pretrained autoencoder, auxiliary detailers, cascaded stages, or U-Net-style skip connections. We systematically examine video patch embeddings, register tokens, and adaptive layer normalization to identify design choices that balance generation quality and computational efficiency. The resulting JVT design achieves generation quality competitive with latent diffusion baselines in both class-conditioned and unconditional settings. The pixel-space formulation of JVT also naturally supports joint generation of pixel-aligned modalities. We demonstrate this capability through camera-conditioned RGB-disparity generation, which improves camera following relative to RGB-only baselines and yields explicit scene geometry without an additional reconstruction model at inference. These results highlight the potential of simple DiT-style Transformers for pixel-space video diffusion, and we hope our findings offer useful insights and practical guidance for future research.","numbers":[],"method":["We introduce JVT (Just Video Transformer), a simple large-patch DiT-style Transformer for pixel-space video diffusion, without a pretrained autoencoder, auxiliary detailers, cascaded stages, or U-Net-style skip connections."],"caveats":[],"topics":["architecture-attention","vision-multimodal","diffusion"],"score":8,"fullText":true,"readChars":5960,"absUrl":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18549","htmlUrl":"https://ar5iv.org/html/2611.18549"}},{"ok":true,"value":{"ok":true,"error":"","id":"2611.16219","title":"GC-MoE on SM120: Overlapping Expert-Parallel Token Dispatch with Warp-Level FP8xFP4 Block-Scaled MMA on Consumer Blackwell","authors":"Yong Li , Haoyu Wang , Kan Liu , Tao Lan","submitted":"12 Nov 2026","abstract":"Expert-parallel Mixture-of-Experts decoding is latency bound: each layer serializes a token exchange, a grouped GEMM over routed experts, and a combine, while only a few tokens per rank are in flight. Prior GC-MoE fused these phases into one persistent, Green-Context-partitioned CUDA Graph on Hopper (SM90) and datacenter Blackwell (SM100). This paper rebuilds the operator for SM120 (compute capability 12.0, e.g. RTX PRO 5000) over PCIe. SM120 exposes block-scaled tensor cores only through warp-level this http URL ::mxf8f6f4: no tensor memory, no two-CTA MMA, no cluster multicast. We rebuild the kernel around a swap-AB warp-level block-scaled MMA with FP4 weights in the register A operand and FP8 activations in B, plus SM120-specific mechanisms: scale-byte selection through MMA immediates, a compact-weights path, an adaptive token-tile geometry, an expert-granular streaming dispatch with a batched bulk-pull engine and a 64-bit deduplicating pull cache, full-width BF16 returns drained by resident relay copiers over a position-major arena, and shared experts folded into the compute role. On 8xRTX PRO 5000 over PCIe the operator is 1.522x and 1.387x faster than the FlashInfer CuTe DSL W4A8 MegaMoE and the Cake MegaMoE across 18 decode points (18/18 wins each), and 1.388x faster than Cake with shared experts fused; all 94 numerical cases and 752 per-rank checks pass, at cross-operator cosine similarity of at least 0.9999997.","numbers":["On 8xRTX PRO 5000 over PCIe the operator is 1.522x and 1.387x faster than the FlashInfer CuTe DSL W4A8 MegaMoE and the Cake MegaMoE across 18 decode points (18/18 wins each), and 1.388x faster than Cake with shared experts fused; all 94 numerical cases and 752 per-rank checks pass, at cross-operator cosine similarity of at least 0.9999997."],"method":[],"caveats":[],"topics":["architecture-attention","quantization"],"score":10,"fullText":true,"readChars":6611,"absUrl":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16219","htmlUrl":"https://ar5iv.org/html/2611.16219"}},{"ok":true,"value":{"ok":true,"error":"","id":"2611.16345","title":"DRAMA: Dissecting Attention Redundancy for Accelerating Multimodal Diffusion Large Language Models","authors":"Yi-Hsin Hung , Fangfu Liu , Shao-Yuan Lo , Chu-Song Chen","submitted":"12 Nov 2026","abstract":"Multimodal diffusion large language models (dLLMs) generate text through iterative masked denoising with bidirectional attention, offering a compelling alternative to autoregressive vision-language modeling. However, each denoising step requires a full forward pass over long multimodal sequences in which visual tokens dominate the sequence length, and a fixed step budget further compounds this quadratic per-step cost. Existing inference acceleration approaches for multimodal dLLMs characterize attention redundancy implicitly, motivating designs from isolated attention visualizations rather than from measurement. We close this gap with DRAMA, a training-free acceleration framework grounded in a systematic dissection of attention redundancy in multimodal dLLMs. By probing attention tensors along spatial and temporal axes, we reveal two persistent redundancy structures: (i) attention mass consistently concentrates on a sparse subset of visual tokens, and (ii) attention distributions rapidly stabilize across denoising steps after an initial formation phase. These findings motivate two complementary mechanisms: Spatial-Redundancy-Aware Visual Token Pruning (SVTP) and Sufficiency-Guided Adaptive Stopping (SGAS). Together, DRAMA achieves an average 18.67 times inference speedup across six vision-language benchmarks without training, architectural modification, or attention caching, while maintaining competitive accuracy. Our results establish a strong, measurement-grounded acceleration baseline for multimodal dLLM inference.","numbers":["Together, DRAMA achieves an average 18.67 times inference speedup across six vision-language benchmarks without training, architectural modification, or attention caching, while maintaining competitive accuracy."],"method":[],"caveats":["However, each denoising step requires a full forward pass over long multimodal sequences in which visual tokens dominate the sequence length, and a fixed step budget further compounds this quadratic per-step cost."],"topics":["architecture-attention","vision-multimodal","diffusion"],"score":12,"fullText":true,"readChars":6777,"absUrl":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16345","htmlUrl":"https://ar5iv.org/html/2611.16345"}},{"ok":true,"value":{"ok":true,"error":"","id":"2611.16500","title":"EvenVLM: A Text and Multimodal Dataset for the Even Language with Vision-Language Fine-Tuning","authors":"Daniil Makovetskii , Ivan Bondarenko","submitted":"12 Nov 2026","abstract":"We present EvenVLM, the first text and multimodal (VQA) dataset for Even (Lamut), an endangered Northern Tungusic language spoken by approximately 6,800 people in Siberia. Our dataset comprises 50,521 structured Even-Russian entries extracted from dictionary and phrasebook sources via GPT-4o Mini-based parsing, from which we synthetically generate 107,543 training examples spanning translation, grammar, reading comprehension, and semantic classification. To address tokenization inefficiency on Even's agglutinative morphology, we propose a three-step tokenizer expansion algorithm that adds Even-specific graphemes (eng, oe, o-umlaut), whitelisted vocabulary, and BPE merges, reducing average tokens per word by 27% (4.99 to 3.64). Using LoRA fine-tuning of Qwen3-VL-4B, we adapt the model for text tasks (0.53% trainable parameters, rank 32) and separately for visual question answering (rank 64, frozen vision encoder). Our best configuration achieves 11.4% exact match and a 13.6 weighted score on text benchmarks, 0.1283 chrF for sentence translation, and 65% accuracy on VQA (up from 0% for the base model and text-only fine-tuning). We release our datasets, evaluation benchmarks, and training pipeline to support further research on Even and other low-resource languages.","numbers":["To address tokenization inefficiency on Even's agglutinative morphology, we propose a three-step tokenizer expansion algorithm that adds Even-specific graphemes (eng, oe, o-umlaut), whitelisted vocabulary, and BPE merges, reducing average tokens per word by 27% (4.99 to 3.64).","Using LoRA fine-tuning of Qwen3-VL-4B, we adapt the model for text tasks (0.53% trainable parameters, rank 32) and separately for visual question answering (rank 64, frozen vision encoder).","Our best configuration achieves 11.4% exact match and a 13.6 weighted score on text benchmarks, 0.1283 chrF for sentence translation, and 65% accuracy on VQA (up from 0% for the base model and text-only fine-tuning)."],"method":["We present EvenVLM, the first text and multimodal (VQA) dataset for Even (Lamut), an endangered Northern Tungusic language spoken by approximately 6,800 people in Siberia.","To address tokenization inefficiency on Even's agglutinative morphology, we propose a three-step tokenizer expansion algorithm that adds Even-specific graphemes (eng, oe, o-umlaut), whitelisted vocabulary, and BPE merges, reducing average tokens per word by 27% (4.99 to 3.64)."],"caveats":[],"topics":["vision-multimodal"],"score":6,"fullText":true,"readChars":6463,"absUrl":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16500","htmlUrl":"https://ar5iv.org/html/2611.16500"}}]}
