{"event":"trap","runId":"briefing-proof3-2026-11-16","trapId":"briefing-proof3-2026-11-16/trap-3","ordinal":3,"type":"job","arg":{"spec":"briefing-render","input":{"date":"2026-11-16","dateLabel":"Monday, November 16, 2026","papers":[{"id":"2611.18482","title":"MMFlash: Faster Speculative Decoding for Vision-Language Models","authors":"Sicheng Feng , Zigeng Chen , Song Wang , Gongfan Fang , Xinchao Wang","submitted":"13 Nov 2026","abstract":"Speculative decoding accelerates generation by letting a lightweight drafter propose tokens for parallel verification by a target model. Diffusion drafters such as DFlash propose an entire token block in a single forward pass, but mainly focus on text-only tasks, leaving multimodal drafting underexplored. To study this setting, we first introduce a metric of token-level visual dependence based on the KL divergence between next-token distributions with and without visual input. We find that acceptance rates decrease from 46% in the lowest visual-dependence quartile to 22% in the highest, with this disparity persisting after multimodal training. Motivated by this observation, we propose MMFlash, a simple and effective framework that adaptively upweights visually dependent tokens that the drafter predicts poorly. For long video inputs, MMFlash further employs sparse attention to reduce redundant attention computation and improve drafting quality. Across various multimodal benchmarks, MMFlash achieves lossless decoding speedups of up to $3.58\\times$, outperforming EAGLE-3 and DFlash by up to 195.4% and 17.1%, respectively.","numbers":["We find that acceptance rates decrease from 46% in the lowest visual-dependence quartile to 22% in the highest, with this disparity persisting after multimodal training.","Across various multimodal benchmarks, MMFlash achieves lossless decoding speedups of up to 3.58, outperforming EAGLE-3 and DFlash by up to 195.4% and 17.1%, respectively."],"method":["Motivated by this observation, we propose MMFlash, a simple and effective framework that adaptively upweights visually dependent tokens that the drafter predicts poorly."],"caveats":[],"relations":[{"priorId":"2609.34832","priorTitle":"BV Loss: Block Verification-Aware Loss for Block Diffusion Speculative Decoding","priorDate":"2026-09-29","basis":"block, verification, diffusion, speculative, decoding","note":"Shares block, verification, diffusion, speculative, decoding with “BV Loss: Block Verification-Aware Loss for Block Diffusion Speculative Decoding” (2026-09-29, arXiv:2609.34832)."},{"priorId":"2610.00623","priorTitle":"HAWK: Rethinking Multimodal Drafting for Speculative Decoding","priorDate":"2026-10-04","basis":"multimodal, drafting, speculative, decoding","note":"Shares multimodal, drafting, speculative, decoding with “HAWK: Rethinking Multimodal Drafting for Speculative Decoding” (2026-10-04, arXiv:2610.00623)."},{"priorId":"2610.06011","priorTitle":"D-Loop: Looped Diffusion Drafting for Speculative Decoding","priorDate":"2026-10-08","basis":"diffusion, drafting, speculative, decoding","note":"Shares diffusion, drafting, speculative, decoding with “D-Loop: Looped Diffusion Drafting for Speculative Decoding” (2026-10-08, arXiv:2610.06011)."}],"threadNote":"","topics":["architecture-attention","vision-multimodal","diffusion","speculative-decoding"],"fullText":true},{"id":"2611.18057","title":"SWAM: Saliency-Weighted Asymmetric Merging for Efficient Vision Transformers","authors":"Utkarsh Kumar Singh , Manish Das , Shiv Ram Dubey , Satish Kumar Singh","submitted":"13 Nov 2026","abstract":"Vision Transformers (ViTs) achieve strong performance on image recognition tasks, yet their quadratic self-attention complexity over input tokens creates prohibitive computational costs for practical deployment. Token reduction methods offer a practical solution by progressively reducing the token sequence during the forward pass. We propose Saliency-Weighted Asymmetric Merging (SWAM), a training-based token merging framework that extends the Bipartite Soft Matching (BSM) with two saliency-aware mechanisms. We introduce a two-step matching formulation in which cosine similarity decides candidate pairs, while a multiplicative maximum saliency penalty regulates their selection for merging. We then further use asymmetric exponential saliency-weighted aggregation, which allows more salient tokens to contribute more strongly to the merged representation. A lightweight MLP-based saliency prediction module (75k parameters per stage, 1% of backbone parameters) supervised via attention rollout is trained end-to-end jointly with a DeiT-Small backbone using a composite cross-entropy, knowledge-distillation, and saliency-alignment objective. On CIFAR-100, TinyImageNet, and ImageNet-100, under a conservative schedule that reduces tokens from 196 to 100 patch tokens, with the CLS token retained throughout, 27.5% FLOPs reduction and 1.35 times speedup, SWAM matches or exceeds baseline across the reported results. Under an aggressive schedule (196 to 70 tokens with the CLS token kept throughout, 33.8% FLOPs reduction, and 1.46 times speedup), accuracy gains are achieved on ImageNet-100 (+0.23%) with minimal losses on CIFAR-100 and TinyImageNet.","numbers":["A lightweight MLP-based saliency prediction module (75k parameters per stage, 1% of backbone parameters) supervised via attention rollout is trained end-to-end jointly with a DeiT-Small backbone using a composite cross-entropy, knowledge-distillation, and saliency-alignment objective.","On CIFAR-100, TinyImageNet, and ImageNet-100, under a conservative schedule that reduces tokens from 196 to 100 patch tokens, with the CLS token retained throughout, 27.5% FLOPs reduction and 1.35 times speedup, SWAM matches or exceeds baseline across the reported results.","Under an aggressive schedule (196 to 70 tokens with the CLS token kept throughout, 33.8% FLOPs reduction, and 1.46 times speedup), accuracy gains are achieved on ImageNet-100 (+0.23%) with minimal losses on CIFAR-100 and TinyImageNet."],"method":["We propose Saliency-Weighted Asymmetric Merging (SWAM), a training-based token merging framework that extends the Bipartite Soft Matching (BSM) with two saliency-aware mechanisms.","We introduce a two-step matching formulation in which cosine similarity decides candidate pairs, while a multiplicative maximum saliency penalty regulates their selection for merging."],"caveats":[],"relations":[{"priorId":"2611.08236","priorTitle":"Beyond Quadratic Attention: Separable Gaussian Operators for ODE Vision Transformers (SSoG)","priorDate":"2026-11-06","basis":"quadratic, attention, vision, transformers","note":"Shares quadratic, attention, vision, transformers with “Beyond Quadratic Attention: Separable Gaussian Operators for ODE Vision Transformers (SSoG)” (2026-11-06, arXiv:2611.08236)."},{"priorId":"2611.03438","priorTitle":"Align Once to Explain: Feature Alignment for Scalable B-cosification of Foundational Vision Transformers","priorDate":"2026-11-04","basis":"alignment, vision, transformers","note":"Shares alignment, vision, transformers with “Align Once to Explain: Feature Alignment for Scalable B-cosification of Foundational Vision Transformers” (2026-11-04, arXiv:2611.03438)."},{"priorId":"2609.34866","priorTitle":"From Attention Sensitivity to Layer Role: Revisiting Mixed-Precision Quantization of Transformers","priorDate":"2026-09-29","basis":"attention, transformers","note":"Shares attention, transformers with “From Attention Sensitivity to Layer Role: Revisiting Mixed-Precision Quantization of Transformers” (2026-09-29, arXiv:2609.34866)."}],"threadNote":"","topics":["architecture-attention","vision-multimodal","diffusion"],"fullText":true},{"id":"2611.08669","title":"ChainFlow: Few-Step Latent Flow Matching for Speculative Decoding","authors":"Selim Aktas , Yusuf Erdem Erdem , Ahmed Zeer , Himmet Toprak Kesgin , Muzaffer Kaan Yuce , Mehmet Fatih Amasyali","submitted":"5 Nov 2026","abstract":"Speculative decoding has become a standard way to accelerate LLM inference: a lightweight drafter proposes tokens that the target verifies in one parallel pass. Existing drafters face trade-offs that limits their speedups: autoregressive drafters generate tokens one at a time, so drafting cost grows with draft length, while diffusion drafters generate tokens in parallel but predict each position independently, ignoring inter-token dependencies. We introduce ChainFlow, a new speculative decoding paradigm and the first flow-matching drafter conditioned on the target's hidden states. ChainFlow treats drafting as a short transport over hidden states, needing one or two flow steps instead of the many denoising steps of generation from noise. A variational autoencoder compresses these states so drafting cost barely grows with target size, lightweight feedback heads restore inter-token dependencies, and tree drafting raises acceptance. On Qwen3.5 models, at matched drafter size and training data, ChainFlow outperforms EAGLE-3 and DFlash in vLLM by 39% and 11%, respectively. These gains further widen as drafters are adapted to new languages sequentially. Our ablations cover drafter design and concurrent serving, and we open-source our code and drafter models.","numbers":["On Qwen3.5 models, at matched drafter size and training data, ChainFlow outperforms EAGLE-3 and DFlash in vLLM by 39% and 11%, respectively."],"method":["We introduce ChainFlow, a new speculative decoding paradigm and the first flow-matching drafter conditioned on the target's hidden states."],"caveats":[],"relations":[{"priorId":"2609.37029","priorTitle":"LongSpark: Efficient speculative decoding with a fixed-cost parallel drafter","priorDate":"2026-10-02","basis":"speculative, decoding, cost, parallel, drafter","note":"Shares speculative, decoding, cost, parallel, drafter with “LongSpark: Efficient speculative decoding with a fixed-cost parallel drafter” (2026-10-02, arXiv:2609.37029)."},{"priorId":"2610.06011","priorTitle":"D-Loop: Looped Diffusion Drafting for Speculative Decoding","priorDate":"2026-10-08","basis":"diffusion, drafting, speculative, decoding","note":"Shares diffusion, drafting, speculative, decoding with “D-Loop: Looped Diffusion Drafting for Speculative Decoding” (2026-10-08, arXiv:2610.06011)."},{"priorId":"2610.33379","priorTitle":"One Call Ahead: Packed Self-Speculative Decoding with Diffusion Drafting","priorDate":"2026-10-31","basis":"speculative, decoding, diffusion, drafting","note":"Shares speculative, decoding, diffusion, drafting with “One Call Ahead: Packed Self-Speculative Decoding with Diffusion Drafting” (2026-10-31, arXiv:2610.33379)."}],"threadNote":"","topics":["diffusion","speculative-decoding"],"fullText":true},{"id":"2611.16345","title":"DRAMA: Dissecting Attention Redundancy for Accelerating Multimodal Diffusion Large Language Models","authors":"Yi-Hsin Hung , Fangfu Liu , Shao-Yuan Lo , Chu-Song Chen","submitted":"12 Nov 2026","abstract":"Multimodal diffusion large language models (dLLMs) generate text through iterative masked denoising with bidirectional attention, offering a compelling alternative to autoregressive vision-language modeling. However, each denoising step requires a full forward pass over long multimodal sequences in which visual tokens dominate the sequence length, and a fixed step budget further compounds this quadratic per-step cost. Existing inference acceleration approaches for multimodal dLLMs characterize attention redundancy implicitly, motivating designs from isolated attention visualizations rather than from measurement. We close this gap with DRAMA, a training-free acceleration framework grounded in a systematic dissection of attention redundancy in multimodal dLLMs. By probing attention tensors along spatial and temporal axes, we reveal two persistent redundancy structures: (i) attention mass consistently concentrates on a sparse subset of visual tokens, and (ii) attention distributions rapidly stabilize across denoising steps after an initial formation phase. These findings motivate two complementary mechanisms: Spatial-Redundancy-Aware Visual Token Pruning (SVTP) and Sufficiency-Guided Adaptive Stopping (SGAS). Together, DRAMA achieves an average 18.67 times inference speedup across six vision-language benchmarks without training, architectural modification, or attention caching, while maintaining competitive accuracy. Our results establish a strong, measurement-grounded acceleration baseline for multimodal dLLM inference.","numbers":["Together, DRAMA achieves an average 18.67 times inference speedup across six vision-language benchmarks without training, architectural modification, or attention caching, while maintaining competitive accuracy."],"method":[],"caveats":["However, each denoising step requires a full forward pass over long multimodal sequences in which visual tokens dominate the sequence length, and a fixed step budget further compounds this quadratic per-step cost."],"relations":[{"priorId":"2611.01320","priorTitle":"Step-dLLM: Adaptive Step-aware Sparse Attention for Efficient Diffusion LLM Inference","priorDate":"2026-11-04","basis":"dllm, adaptive, sparse, attention, diffusion, inference","note":"Shares dllm, adaptive, sparse, attention, diffusion, inference with “Step-dLLM: Adaptive Step-aware Sparse Attention for Efficient Diffusion LLM Inference” (2026-11-04, arXiv:2611.01320)."},{"priorId":"2609.19868","priorTitle":"Zarya: A Hybrid Autoregressive–Masked Diffusion Language Model with Flexible Training and Dual-Mode Inference","priorDate":"2026-10-01","basis":"autoregressive, masked, diffusion, inference","note":"Shares autoregressive, masked, diffusion, inference with “Zarya: A Hybrid Autoregressive–Masked Diffusion Language Model with Flexible Training and Dual-Mode Inference” (2026-10-01, arXiv:2609.19868)."},{"priorId":"2610.08738","priorTitle":"Denoising Hierarchical Representations: Joint Continuous Diffusion for Language Modeling","priorDate":"2026-10-07","basis":"denoising, diffusion, modeling","note":"Shares denoising, diffusion, modeling with “Denoising Hierarchical Representations: Joint Continuous Diffusion for Language Modeling” (2026-10-07, arXiv:2610.08738)."}],"threadNote":"","topics":["architecture-attention","vision-multimodal","diffusion"],"fullText":true},{"id":"2611.11320","title":"Beyond Similarity Entropy: A Rank-Guided Data-Free Quantization Framework for Vision Transformers","authors":"Runqing Jiang , Ye Zhang , Pengpeng Yu , Zhuo Song , Changjun Li , Longguang Wang , Yulan Guo","submitted":"8 Nov 2026","abstract":"Vision Transformers (ViTs) have demonstrated remarkable performance, yet their computational and memory demands pose significant challenges for deployment on mobile devices. Data-free quantization (DFQ) enables the compression of ViTs without access to their original training data. Existing DFQ methods often synthesize calibration samples by maximizing the entropy of patch similarity. However, similarity entropy describes the dispersion of similarity values without ensuring that the generated samples cover diverse feature directions, potentially limiting their effectiveness for quantization calibration. To address this limitation, we propose RankQ-ViT, a rank-guided DFQ framework for ViTs. We first observe that patch similarity matrices derived from Gaussian noise tend to have lower ranks than those derived from real images. Motivated by this observation, we introduce a block-aware self-contrastive objective that promotes feature diversity during synthetic data generation without explicitly computing matrix rank at each optimization step. We then select calibration samples based on their rank and semantic consistency and use them for block-wise reconstruction of the quantized model. Experiments on ImageNet, COCO, ModelNet40, and ShapeNet demonstrate the effectiveness of RankQ-ViT across multiple architectures and tasks, particularly under low-bit quantization. The code is available at this https URL","numbers":[],"method":["To address this limitation, we propose RankQ-ViT, a rank-guided DFQ framework for ViTs.","Motivated by this observation, we introduce a block-aware self-contrastive objective that promotes feature diversity during synthetic data generation without explicitly computing matrix rank at each optimization step."],"caveats":["However, similarity entropy describes the dispersion of similarity values without ensuring that the generated samples cover diverse feature directions, potentially limiting their effectiveness for quantization calibration.","To address this limitation, we propose RankQ-ViT, a rank-guided DFQ framework for ViTs."],"relations":[{"priorId":"2610.00983","priorTitle":"The Devil Is in the Reconstruction Loss Scale: Rethinking Optimization in LLM Quantization","priorDate":"2026-10-02","basis":"reconstruction, optimization, quantization","note":"Shares reconstruction, optimization, quantization with “The Devil Is in the Reconstruction Loss Scale: Rethinking Optimization in LLM Quantization” (2026-10-02, arXiv:2610.00983)."},{"priorId":"2610.02953","priorTitle":"SlimKV: Joint Token-Feature KV Cache Compression with Reconstruction-Free Beacon Attention","priorDate":"2026-10-05","basis":"feature, compression, reconstruction, free","note":"Shares feature, compression, reconstruction, free with “SlimKV: Joint Token-Feature KV Cache Compression with Reconstruction-Free Beacon Attention” (2026-10-05, arXiv:2610.02953)."},{"priorId":"2610.12448","priorTitle":"One Block, Multiple Depths: Recurrent Vision Transformers with Depth-Programmed Experts (reViT)","priorDate":"2026-10-11","basis":"block, multiple, vision, transformers","note":"Shares block, multiple, vision, transformers with “One Block, Multiple Depths: Recurrent Vision Transformers with Depth-Programmed Experts (reViT)” (2026-10-11, arXiv:2610.12448)."}],"threadNote":"","topics":["architecture-attention","vision-multimodal","quantization"],"fullText":true},{"id":"2611.14196","title":"Joint Quantization and Dynamic Partitioning for Context-Aware Edge Vision Transformers","authors":"Zhu Jiaqi , Jain Prakriti , Katare Dewant , Kumar Saurabh","submitted":"10 Nov 2026","abstract":"Deploying vision transformer models across connected and automated vehicles (CAVs) requires joint management of computation and Vehicle-to-Vehicle (V2V) communication. Static split-inference methods use fixed partition points and cannot adapt to time-varying link bandwidth, processing load, and concurrent multi-stream traffic. This paper proposes an edge orchestration framework that jointly selects model partition points, per-layer quantization bitwidths, and execution placement across heterogeneous vehicular compute nodes. The joint partitioning and mixed-precision allocation problem is formulated as a Mixed-Integer Linear Program (MILP) that minimizes compute and communication energy subject to end-to-end latency and task-quality constraints. The framework is evaluated on Jetson Orin Nano (8~GB), Jetson Orin NX (16~GB), and Jetson AGX Orin (32~GB) nodes using OPV2V-driven multi-stream workloads with $K \\in \\{2,4,6,8\\}$. Across the evaluated configurations, the proposed formulation reduces V2V communication energy by $33.3$--$36.0\\%$ relative to a Uniform INT8 split. At $K=8$, end-to-end task completion latency is reduced by $34.0\\%$ on the Orin Nano and $33.1\\%$ on the Orin NX relative to the same baseline, while limiting the reduction in 3D detection accuracy to $3.1$--$4.1$ percentage points in $\\mathrm{mAP}_{50}$ relative to FP16 execution.","numbers":["Across the evaluated configurations, the proposed formulation reduces V2V communication energy by 33.3–36.0% relative to a Uniform INT8 split.","At K=8, end-to-end task completion latency is reduced by 34.0% on the Orin Nano and 33.1% on the Orin NX relative to the same baseline, while limiting the reduction in 3D detection accuracy to 3.1–4.1 percentage points in mAP_50 relative to FP16 execution."],"method":["This paper proposes an edge orchestration framework that jointly selects model partition points, per-layer quantization bitwidths, and execution placement across heterogeneous vehicular compute nodes."],"caveats":[],"relations":[{"priorId":"2609.34866","priorTitle":"From Attention Sensitivity to Layer Role: Revisiting Mixed-Precision Quantization of Transformers","priorDate":"2026-09-29","basis":"layer, mixed, precision, quantization, transformers","note":"Shares layer, mixed, precision, quantization, transformers with “From Attention Sensitivity to Layer Role: Revisiting Mixed-Precision Quantization of Transformers” (2026-09-29, arXiv:2609.34866)."},{"priorId":"2610.19605","priorTitle":"PN-Mix: Pair-Norm Guided Mixed-Precision Key Cache Quantization for Efficient Long-Output LLM Inference","priorDate":"2026-10-17","basis":"mixed, precision, quantization, inference","note":"Shares mixed, precision, quantization, inference with “PN-Mix: Pair-Norm Guided Mixed-Precision Key Cache Quantization for Efficient Long-Output LLM Inference” (2026-10-17, arXiv:2610.19605)."},{"priorId":"2611.10096","priorTitle":"CoMixKV: Coordinated Mixed-Precision KV Cache Quantization for Efficient LLM-based Multi-agent Systems","priorDate":"2026-11-15","basis":"mixed, precision, quantization, multi","note":"Shares mixed, precision, quantization, multi with “CoMixKV: Coordinated Mixed-Precision KV Cache Quantization for Efficient LLM-based Multi-agent Systems” (2026-11-15, arXiv:2611.10096)."}],"threadNote":"","topics":["architecture-attention","vision-multimodal","quantization"],"fullText":true},{"id":"2611.17930","title":"Token2Tile: Token-Wise Sparse Attention with Tile Execution through a Low-Rank Space for Video DiTs","authors":"Xuecheng Wu , Aichen Feng , Jinyan Chen , Qingjie Zhao , Julien Lai","submitted":"13 Nov 2026","abstract":"Attention is essential for modeling spatio-temporal dependencies in video diffusion transformers, but its quadratic cost makes it a major inference bottleneck. Its high sparsity offers an opportunity to accelerate generation by focusing computation on important interactions. However, accuracy and execution favor different granularities: each query attends to its own key tokens, whereas GPU kernels work on tiles. We present Token2Tile, a training-free sparse attention operator that combines per-query scoring and a per-query residual with tile execution. An online low-rank space, computed directly from queries and keys to preserve attention logits, makes clustering, scoring, and residual approximation efficient. Token2Tile unites query-specific selections to allocate exact work across query clusters, then packs the selected keys into GPU tiles. On MiniMax-H3 and Wan2.1-14B, Token2Tile delivers 1.48-1.54x end-to-end speedup over dense FlashAttention-3 with VBench scores within 0.5 points of dense. At comparable speeds on H3, it improves PSNR over SOL and SubBlock by 5.5-6.8 dB; on Wan, it exceeds SOL by 4.6-4.8 dB while running faster.","numbers":["On MiniMax-H3 and Wan2.1-14B, Token2Tile delivers 1.48-1.54x end-to-end speedup over dense FlashAttention-3 with VBench scores within 0.5 points of dense.","At comparable speeds on H3, it improves PSNR over SOL and SubBlock by 5.5-6.8 dB; on Wan, it exceeds SOL by 4.6-4.8 dB while running faster."],"method":["We present Token2Tile, a training-free sparse attention operator that combines per-query scoring and a per-query residual with tile execution."],"caveats":["However, accuracy and execution favor different granularities: each query attends to its own key tokens, whereas GPU kernels work on tiles."],"relations":[{"priorId":"2610.06801","priorTitle":"MC-Sparse: Deconstructing and Closing the Dense–Sparse Attention Gap in Diffusion Transformers — arXiv:2610.06801","priorDate":"2026-10-15","basis":"sparse, dense, attention, diffusion, transformers","note":"Shares sparse, dense, attention, diffusion, transformers with “MC-Sparse: Deconstructing and Closing the Dense–Sparse Attention Gap in Diffusion Transformers — arXiv:2610.06801” (2026-10-15, arXiv:2610.06801)."},{"priorId":"2610.35706","priorTitle":"Triage: Skip, Skim, or Refine Attention Tiles in Video Diffusion Transformers","priorDate":"2026-10-27","basis":"attention, tiles, video, diffusion, transformers","note":"Shares attention, tiles, video, diffusion, transformers with “Triage: Skip, Skim, or Refine Attention Tiles in Video Diffusion Transformers” (2026-10-27, arXiv:2610.35706)."},{"priorId":"2611.01320","priorTitle":"Step-dLLM: Adaptive Step-aware Sparse Attention for Efficient Diffusion LLM Inference","priorDate":"2026-11-04","basis":"sparse, attention, diffusion, inference","note":"Shares sparse, attention, diffusion, inference with “Step-dLLM: Adaptive Step-aware Sparse Attention for Efficient Diffusion LLM Inference” (2026-11-04, arXiv:2611.01320)."}],"threadNote":"","topics":["architecture-attention","vision-multimodal","diffusion"],"fullText":true}],"mustReadId":"2611.18482","scopeNote":"**TEST EDITION — produced by the AgentTS briefing workflow (BRA-55 proof run), not the live 06:15 cron.** Covers arXiv submissions 2026-11-09–2026-11-16 in cs.LG, cs.CL, cs.CV, cs.AI, stat.ML, cs.NE, deduped against a copy of the seen list (2892 papers scanned, 50 seen IDs excluded). All 7 papers below were verified on their own arXiv abs pages, with full-text reads where ar5iv served the paper; every number and claim is an extracted sentence from the fetched text. The must-read is 2611.18482."}}}
